latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Input Analysis:\n   a) Divide the 19x4 input grid into four sections, ignoring the green (3) separator columns.\n   b) For each section:\n      - Count the frequency of each color (excluding black and green).\n      - Identify colors that appear in clusters.\n      - Calculate the distribution entropy (how evenly spread or clustered the colors are).\n   c) Create a global color frequency count across all sections.\n\n2. Color Importance Ranking:\n   a) Assign an importance score to each color based on:\n      - Overall frequency in the input.\n      - Frequency of appearing as a dominant color in individual sections.\n      - Tendency to appear in clusters.\n   b) Create a sorted list of colors by their importance score.\n\n3. Output Grid Generation:\n   a) Initialize a 4x4 output grid.\n   b) For each column in the output grid:\n      - Identify the most important color from the corresponding input section.\n      - Place this color in at least two cells of the column.\n      - Fill the remaining cells with the second most important color or a color that creates a diagonal pattern with other columns.\n\n4. Pattern Creation:\n   a) Identify the top 2-3 most important colors.\n   b) Create at least one diagonal pattern using one of these colors.\n   c) Ensure that no more than two cells of the same color are adjacent (horizontally or vertically).\n\n5. Representation Balancing:\n   a) Check if all key colors from the input are represented in the output.\n   b) If any key color is missing, replace a less important color with it.\n   c) Ensure that the color distribution in the output roughly matches the input.\n\n6. Entropy Representation:\n   a) Identify sections in the input with high color entropy (many different colors or lots of black).\n   b) Represent these high-entropy areas in the output using black (0) or a mix of less frequent colors.\n\n7. Symmetry and Aesthetics:\n   a) Look for opportunities to create symmetry in the output grid without contradicting the input representation.\n   b) Ensure that corners and edges of the output grid accurately represent dominant colors or patterns from the input.\n\n8. Fine-tuning:\n   a) Double-check that the output grid balances accurate representation with visual appeal.\n   b) Make small adjustments to enhance diagonal patterns or symmetry if they don't contradict the input representation.\n   c) Ensure that no single color dominates the entire output unless it overwhelmingly dominates the input.\n\n9. Validation:\n   a) Compare the generated output against the input to ensure it's a fair representation.\n   b) Verify that the output maintains the key characteristics observed in the example outputs (balance of accuracy and aesthetics).\n\n10. Iteration:\n    If the output doesn't satisfy the balance between input representation and aesthetic patterns, return to step 3 and try alternative color placements or pattern formations.\n\nThis plan aims to capture both the accurate representation of the input and the creation of visually appealing patterns in the output, as observed in the example cases. It allows for flexibility in handling different input distributions while maintaining consistency in the output generation process."
